- op: create  # 1ee4
  at: '2026-04-07T10:13:08Z'  # 1ee4
  by: agent  # 1ee4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1ee4
  clock: 1  # 1ee4
  nonce: 1ee4  # 1ee4
  data:  # 1ee4
    kind: work_item  # 1ee4
    title: "Tier classifier: in-tree *_test.go files should be tier 1, not tier 2"  # 1ee4
    status: todo_soft  # 1ee4
    priority: 3  # 1ee4
    parent:  # 1ee4
    tags:  # 1ee4
    - analysis_quality  # 1ee4
    - go  # 1ee4
    before: []  # 1ee4
    duplicate_of: []  # 1ee4
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 1ee4
    pr_ref:  # 1ee4
    description: "Surfaced from deep-20260407-050552 reflect aggregate (alertmanager + prometheus). The tier classifier is currently classifying in-tree Go test files (config/coordinator_test.go, dispatch/dispatch_test.go, notify/notify_test.go, provider/mem/mem_test.go, silence/silence_test.go, test/cli/mock.go, test/testutils/{acceptance,collector}.go on alertmanager; api_test.go, manager_test.go, xds_test.go, file_test.go, query_log_test.go on prometheus) as tier=2. They should be tier=1 because they live in the same Go module (same go.mod) as the production code.\\n\\nImpact: tier-2 inclusion in slices is supposed to reveal genuine third-party dependencies for the user. With this bug, the 14 tier-2 nodes alertmanager exposes are all in-tree test fixtures, not cgo deps or vendored libs, making the tier-2 slice useless for 'what packages do I depend on?' answers. Same on prometheus (24 tier-2 nodes, all _test.go).\\n\\nRoot cause to investigate: the supply_chain tier classifier in core/supply_chain.py — likely conflates 'is_test_file' with 'is_external_module'. The Go-specific path probably puts test files into tier 2 because they aren't in 'main package execution', but tier and test-ness are orthogonal axes that should not be merged.\\n\\nReproduce: `hypergumbo run /home/jgstern_agent/repos/alertmanager`, then inspect any *_test.go node's supply_chain_tier field. Should be 1; currently 2.\\n\\nThis is a different (opposite) issue from WI-kodas (which was about Vue templates being correctly tier 1 — that one was metric interpretation, not classification). This one is genuine misclassification."  # 1ee4
    fields: {}  # 1ee4
- op: discuss  # 90cc
  at: '2026-04-07T21:37:39Z'  # 90cc
  by: agent  # 90cc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 90cc
  clock: 2  # 90cc
  nonce: 90cc  # 90cc
  message: "Investigated this — the architectural impact is wider than the WI description suggests, so I want to confirm direction before charging ahead.\n\nCURRENT STATE (verified by reading packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/supply_chain.py + tests):\n\nThe current design is *strongly committed* to 'tests = tier 2'. It is asserted by ~20 explicit unit tests in test_supply_chain.py::TestTestFileClassification, all of which carry the docstring 'Test code is not production code — it should not be tier 1 (first_party).' Languages covered: Python tests/, Ruby spec/, Jest __tests__/, Maven src/test/java/, Go _test.go, JS .test.js, TS .spec.ts, Ruby _spec.rb, Rust tests.rs and testonly.rs, C++ unit_tests/ and test_*.cpp/cc. There is a similar TestFuzzBenchClassification block treating fuzz/bench as tier 2 with the same rationale.\n\nThe classify_file function has TWO test-detection branches that would need to flip:\n  (1) lines 304-314: when a file is inside a workspace package_root, test patterns demote to INTERNAL_DEP (tier 2) before the workspace-FIRST_PARTY branch. Removing this would put workspace test files into FIRST_PARTY.\n  (2) lines 326-331: when a file is NOT in a workspace package_root, test patterns are checked before FIRST_PARTY default. Removing this would route tests to FIRST_PARTY via the default branch.\n\nDOWNSTREAM CONSUMERS (verified by grepping slice.py and test_max_tier.py):\n- slice.py:614 reads node.supply_chain_tier when filtering by max_tier. Workflow 'give me production code only' currently uses max_tier=1 and gets a clean cut. After flipping, max_tier=1 would include test files — every consumer that wants 'real production code only' must add an is_test_file filter.\n- test_max_tier.py and test_slice_tier_filter.py have explicit assertions that tier=2 includes test directories.\n- centrality.py inherits the same bug (consumes tier indirectly via filtered slices).\n- bakeoff-deep tier-bounded slice queries (the very thing that surfaced this WI) currently expect tier=2 to be 'tests + internal_dep mixed'.\n\nARCHITECTURAL CHOICE: there are two ways to honor your directive 'tier and test-ness are orthogonal axes that should not be merged'.\n\nOPTION A (the literal reading of your directive): flip the classification so in-tree test files are tier 1, update all ~20 unit tests, and audit every consumer of 'tier=1 only' workflows. Highest payoff for tier-2-as-real-deps queries; highest blast radius. Estimated scope: supply_chain.py + ~20 test updates + slice.py max_tier consumer changes + bakeoff-deep tier-slice query changes + CHANGELOG/spec updates. Probably 3-5 files plus extensive test edits.\n\nOPTION B (less invasive, semantically equivalent for your stated bakeoff use case): add an is_test boolean to FileClassification (and propagate to node.is_test_file). Keep the existing tier classification untouched. The bakeoff-deep tier-bounded slice query becomes 'tier=2 AND NOT is_test' and you get the same answer ('what packages do I depend on?'). All existing tests stay green; the change is purely additive. Cost: bakeoff queries (and any user-facing workflow that wants 'real deps') must add the filter. Benefit: no consumer has to relearn what tier means. The 'orthogonal axes' you describe become genuinely orthogonal — but tier remains a multi-purpose code-quality + supply-chain hybrid.\n\nOPTION C (compromise): introduce a new tier for in-tree tests (e.g., tier 1.5 = 'first-party non-production', or rename existing tier 2 = 'internal-but-tests' vs new tier 'internal-deps-from-other-packages'). Most semantically clean; biggest schema change. Requires updating SCHEMA_VERSION and Tier enum. Probably overkill.\n\nMY RECOMMENDATION: Option B is the smallest change that solves your stated bakeoff problem without introducing regressions. The is_test field already exists conceptually (the test patterns ARE detected), it just gets folded into tier instead of being exposed as a separate field. Surfacing it adds an axis without removing one.\n\nQUESTIONS FOR YOU before I implement:\n1. Does Option B (add is_test,  # 90cc
    keep tier) actually solve your underlying need, or do you specifically want 'tier=1' to mean 'tests live here' going forward?\n2. If Option A: am I right to update the ~20 existing TestTestFileClassification tests by inverting them ('test files are tier 1, with is_test=True')? Or should those tests be deleted entirely?\n3. Are there other consumers I should audit beyond slice.py max_tier and bakeoff-deep tier-slice queries?\n\nI have NOT made any code changes yet. Holding for your direction."  # 90cc
