  message: "re (b) -- well actually this item, WI-rigun, was created by *you* and not me! so the question is not what I was thinking but what *you* were thinking! Or if you don't recall, what you think you might have reasonably been thinking! But you could look at the transcript logs to understand more of the context from when your wrote the description."  # d32c
- op: discuss  # ad09
  at: '2026-04-09T00:01:12Z'  # ad09
  by: agent  # ad09
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ad09
  clock: 8  # ad09
  nonce: ad09  # ad09
  message: "INVESTIGATION COMPLETE on both (a) and (b).\n\n(a) WHY 'test code = tier 2': Found the originating commit. 89154fa20 'feat: classify test files as tier 2 (internal_dep) in supply chain' (2026-02-12, jgstern-agent). Commit message: 'Test directories... are now classified as tier 2 instead of defaulting to tier 1. The check runs before first-party path matching... Previously, 27/28 repos in the DEEP bakeoff showed 97-100% tier1 because test files were counted as production code.' So the original motivation was the EXACT MIRROR IMAGE of WI-rigun: at T1 the bakeoff complained 'tier1 is meaningless because tests inflate it'; at T2 (this WI) the bakeoff complains 'tier2 is meaningless because tests pollute it'. Both at the time felt obviously correct. The 20+ TestTestFileClassification tests with the docstring 'Test code is not production code' encode the T1 framing.\n\n(b) WHY *I* wrote 'tier and test-ness are orthogonal axes': checked the transcript (246a2da5 line 2887, 2026-04-07T10:13:08Z). Context: I was investigating spurious tier-2 nodes in deep-20260407-050552 alertmanager (14 of them), running ad-hoc python scripts against hg.json, found they were ALL in-tree _test.go files. The bakeoff use case 'tier-2 should reveal third-party deps' was being defeated. I wrote the WI in a single tool-use burst without consulting prior history — the 'orthogonal axes' framing is me reaching for a clean conceptual rule that solved the immediate symptom, but I had not checked whether tier-2-as-tests had a contemporaneous justification (it did — see (a)).\n\nSYNTHESIS: the two framings are not actually contradictory. They are both consequences of cramming two independent dimensions ('first-party vs deps' AND 'production vs tests') into a single ordinal tier field. Each consumer wanted ONE of those dimensions to dominate. Both are right inside their own bakeoff query. The structural answer is the same one I proposed as Option B in my earlier message: add an explicit is_test boolean to FileClassification (and propagate to node.is_test_file), keep the existing tier classification, and let consumers compose. 'Production code only' = max_tier=1 AND NOT is_test. 'Real third-party deps' = tier=2 AND NOT is_test. Both bakeoff queries get clean answers; neither historical decision needs to be unwound; the ~20 TestTestFileClassification tests stay green because tier semantics are unchanged.\n\nThis is also the cheapest path forward. Option A would require unwinding 89154fa20's intent and updating those tests; Option C (new tier value) would require schema bump. Option B is purely additive.\n\nACTION: I recommend reframing this WI as 'Add is_test boolean to FileClassification, expose as node.is_test_file, update bakeoff-deep tier-slice queries to compose with it'. Title can change to reflect the additive framing. Awaiting your approval before changing the title/scope."  # ad09
